@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel)
===================================================

APB - All Points Bulletin (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run
natively: the arcade board's two processors' programs translated into
C++ and compiled for your Mac, checked against MAME's processor
cores, drawn with Vulkan
(through MoltenVK, which comes with the app).

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the ROMs:
@RELEASE@ (136051-1101.5t to 136051-7129.6n),
unzipped into a folder of their own.
MAME's apb.zip is that set zipped: double-click it in Finder to unzip it.


Playing
-------
1. Drag @TITLE@.app onto Applications (in this disk image's window). Building
   the game needs about 1 GB free.
2. Open @TITLE@ from Applications. The first time, macOS stops it (the app
   isn't signed by an Apple developer account, see below): open System
   Settings, Privacy & Security, scroll down to "@TITLE@ was blocked", click
   Open Anyway and confirm with your password. macOS asks only once.
3. The game is compiled with Apple's own compiler, free from Apple (its
   licence doesn't let it come with this download). If your Mac doesn't have
   it yet, the app says so: click "Install Apple's command line tools",
   follow Apple's installer (a few minutes), then click "Check again".
4. Click "Choose your ROM set" and pick the folder holding the ROM files,
   or drag the folder onto the window. It tells you straight away if files
   are missing or damaged, or if it is another revision of APB.
5. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. Your ROMs never leave your Mac.
6. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs.

After that, the app remembers your ROM folder, and Play starts the game
straight away. After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling
only what changed.

The game's output is in run.log and run-errors.log, and the build's in
build.log, all in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@/work (in Finder:
Go, Go to Folder). Send them when reporting a problem.

About "Open Anyway": the app is signed only by itself ("ad hoc"), not by an
Apple developer account, so Gatekeeper can't name who made it. Check that you
downloaded it from the project's GitHub releases page (below) before you
open it. Nothing in it changes that check or works around it.

Needs: macOS 14.5 (Sonoma) or newer, on an Apple silicon or Intel Mac, and
Apple's command line tools from Xcode 16 or newer, which need that macOS
(the app checks; older tools are updated with System Settings, General,
Software Update). Graphics go through MoltenVK, Vulkan over Apple's Metal.


Controls
--------
Keyboard: arrows steer and accelerate; Left Control, Left Option, Space are
the siren/start, fire and alternate siren; 5 inserts a coin. Gamepad: left
stick or d-pad steers, right trigger or d-pad up accelerates, A or Start
starts/sirens, B fires, X is alternate siren, Back inserts a coin.

F1 or Esc (gamepad: Back + Start together): the settings menu (Page Up /
Page Down or LB / RB change its pages): wheels, pedals, buttons, the
picture (presets, size, a CRT look, smoothed pixel art), full screen, the
volume. F3: frame times. F11, Option+Return or the window's green button:
full screen. On a Mac keyboard the F keys may need the fn key. Wheels: a
direct-drive (free-turning) wheel, a mouse or a spinner; see the Controls
page.

Settings and high scores are kept in ~/Library/Application
Support/qilin/@GAME@, with the ROM folder you chose, the build (work) and
its cache of compiled files (build-cache).

Checking a wheel and performance: once the game is built, start it in
Terminal with an input recording, performance timings and a controller
report (the first line finds the app's MoltenVK), play for a couple of
minutes and quit normally:
  export DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH=/Applications/@TITLE@.app/Contents/Frameworks
  cd ~/Library/Application\ Support/qilin/@GAME@
  work/@EXE@ --record check.qir --profile performance.txt \
      --controller-report controllers.txt
controllers.txt includes serials and device paths; the recording includes
the starting high scores and settings, but no ROMs.


What's in the app
-----------------
Right-click @TITLE@.app, Show Package Contents:

Contents/MacOS/Play @TITLE@           chooses your ROM folder, builds the
                                  game (once) and starts it
Contents/Resources/kit/qilin      the recompiler: turns the ROMs' programs
                                  into C++
Contents/Resources/kit/lib, include
                                  the prebuilt rest of the game: the
                                  board, video, sound, the window and
                                  controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
Contents/Resources/kit/files.txt  every file this release holds (see below)
Contents/Frameworks/libMoltenVK.dylib
                                  MoltenVK: Vulkan over Metal (Khronos)
Contents/Resources/LICENSES       the licences of everything included

The game itself (@EXE@), built from your ROMs, is in the work folder above,
not in the app: the app is never changed by building.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/Qilin1066/ApbArcade--QilinRecomp


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in the app asks GitHub for a newer release (it sends
nothing about you or your Mac). If there is one, it downloads the disk
image, copies the new app beside this one and swaps it in, then starts
again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on the
way, the old version is left as it was. The app must be somewhere you can
write to for that (Applications is, for an administrator): run from the disk
image itself, it can't update. You can also drag a new download's app over
this one in Applications: it finds your ROM folder and the cache by itself.
Your settings and high scores are never in the app, so nothing of yours is
touched.

The game is compiled for this Mac's processor; on another Mac, it is built
again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made from has
changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one before a
new build is kept as work/@EXE@.previous until the new one has run and
closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
